1. Introduction

1.1 Background

Traditional rule development processes within credit strategy environments have historically relied on manual analytical workflows involving feature investigation, threshold testing, segment analysis, rule construction, and governance review.

While effective, increasing data complexity has introduced several challenges.

Firstly, decision making increasingly incorporates multiple information sources including bureau data, multi-bureau data, behavioural information, engineered variables, and external datasets. The expansion of available features significantly increases analytical complexity and makes manual investigation increasingly time intensive.

Secondly, traditional workflows typically explore only a limited number of feature combinations and threshold structures. As dimensionality increases, exhaustive exploration becomes impractical, increasing the possibility that stronger candidate rules remain unidentified.

Thirdly, after predictive variables are identified, additional validation activities are usually required to ensure logical consistency, expected risk relationships, and governance compliance. Historically these activities relied heavily on manual review and became increasingly difficult to scale when transitioning from single-bureau to multi-bureau environments.

Finally, modern rule development requires not only predictive capability but also explainability, interpretability, and governance readiness. Pure machine learning outputs often require additional interpretation before implementation.

PRAISE (Policy Rule AI Strategy Engine) was developed to address these challenges.

The framework combines statistical analysis, machine learning methods, explainable AI techniques, automated validation procedures, and optimisation approaches to support governance-ready rule construction.

The framework is divided into three major sections:

* Preparation
* Section A – Feature Selection
* Section B – Rule Construction

